Subject: [PATCH 07998/11362] [outer-loop] FIX THE GENERATOR CONCURRENCY FAULT:
 n_ctx 16384 -> 81920, and the scored path finally gets a liveness witness

User instruction: "fix the concurrency fault", after two operator decisions --
"measure per-slot VRAM first, then fix", and "fix the concurrency fault first,
then decide" the strategic pivot. Both honoured: the envelope was measured before
anything shipped, and no pivot is recommended here.

THE FAULT. llama-server with no explicit --parallel sets n_parallel=4 AND
kv_unified=true (server.cpp:106-110), so the 4 slots share ONE pool of -c cells.
Admission requires n_ctx >= K_concurrent * (prompt + max_tokens). At the shipped
16384 with max_tokens=4096 it fired at K=2, not K=4 (2 x (5968+4096) = 20128 >
16384). Keeping 16384 is ARITHMETICALLY IMPOSSIBLE: 16384/4 - 15734 is negative,
so NO max_tokens fits the worst real prompt (grids span 2x2..64x64, so the induce
prompt spans 1411..15754 tokens and the failing K is per-game).

THREE DISTINCT MODES; conflating them would have fixed the wrong one.
  A. large prompt, K>=2 -> HTTP 500 "Context size has been exceeded", SURVIVES.
  B. small prompts admitted, generations overrun the pool -> RemoteDisconnected,
     server DIES PERMANENTLY at common/sampling.cpp:154
     GGML_ASSERT(logits != nullptr) -> ggml_abort inside update_slots().
  C. prompt nearly fills the pool -> HTTP 200 with SILENT TRUNCATION.
The 2026-07-26 probe hit B and reported "the server died"; the investigation
probed with a large prompt, hit A, and refuted that. Both were right about
different modes.

THE FIX: LocalGGUFProposer.n_ctx 16384 -> 81920 via
field(default_factory=_default_induce_n_ctx), so the literal lives in ONE place
and is overridable with CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_N_CTX. No --parallel. From
4 x (15734+4096) = 79320. Costs +1668 MiB (13763 vs 12095); n_ctx_train=262144.
Both LocalGGUFProposer construction sites omit n_ctx and so cannot diverge -- the
REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-35 comment records that those two ALREADY diverged once, for
max_tokens.

THE LOAD-BEARING RESULT: THE CHEAPER FIX WOULD HAVE SHIPPED THE BUG. --parallel 1
passes an HTTP gate 4/4 AT LOWER VRAM, but generated 648/650/184/648 tokens
against a 4096 budget -- converting the loud 500 into mode C, the exact defect
under investigation. Had the gate been HTTP-status-only it would have PASSED AND
SHIPPED. So the gate carries a stop_taxonomy witness separating
intended_budget_limit (gen == max_tokens) from pool_exhaustion_limit (gen <<
max_tokens with stop=limit) -- a distinction the shipped agent conflated. Every
fix request reported predicted_n == 4096 == max_tokens; pool_exhaustion_limit == 0
in every cell.

VERIFIED THROUGH THE SHIPPED LAUNCH PATH, not a hand-built command line. exp5866
priced the envelope with its own llama-server invocation, which CANNOT verify a
change to LocalGGUFProposer because it never executes it. The fix arm launches via
_ensure_server(). Control in the same tree, same prompt, same binary, back-to-back:
control 2/2 and 4/4 HTTP 500; fix 2/2 and 4/4 HTTP 200. Failure SET: control 6
cells, fix 0, new_failures_introduced_by_fix: []. Gate non-forced (control failed
6/6). The +1668 MiB price replicated to the MiB across two independent launch paths.

SILENT DEGRADATION FIXED IN THE RECORD, NOT THE CONTROL FLOW. generate() still
does not raise, deliberately: raising today would be strictly LESS informative
(the outermost induce handler at :5985 was a bare except discarding
type/message/traceback), aborting cannot GAIN score (an unsolved level scores 0
regardless), and swarm.py:91 runs one Thread per game in ONE process, so one dead
server would zero EVERY game. Instead: proposer counters at the choke point all 11
call sites funnel through; repr(exc) at :5985; _describe_http_failure() now reads
the response BODY (the 400's "try increasing it" was LITERALLY the fix, read and
discarded twice); _limit_diagnostic() separates pool truncation from budget limit
(the old message said "HIT n_predict" for both, and the prescriptions are
OPPOSITE).

A STRUCTURALLY DEAD CHANNEL, worse than an empty one: the census found 877 stat
blocks carrying an errors key and NOT ONE non-zero value, including all 8
provably-dead cells. The field existed, was read, and could never report anything.
After the fix a dead generator yields errors=2 for 2 calls, server-vs-content
failures kept separate so an answering-but-terse server still counts as alive.

THE SCORED PATH HAD NO OBSERVABILITY AT ALL: zero print and zero logging in 6290
lines, and induction_attempts died with the game's thread. Added
E3AgentPolicy.generator_liveness_witness() + a CarnotAgent.cleanup() override (the
framework's once-per-game hook, super().cleanup() in a finally): one greppable
"LLM LIVENESS ..." stderr line that survives a read-only filesystem, plus a JSON
row under CARNOT_ARC_LIVENESS_DIR. This is the gap the kernel author named at
submission_kernel/main.py:68 and left open. The kernel pre-flight also imported a
hardcoded -c "16384" and probed with /health -- which IS a K=1 probe, the exact
blind spot -- and now imports _default_induce_n_ctx() and probes 2 concurrent
full-budget requests.

GUARD PROVEN ON ITS ORIGIN INCIDENT (the failure this project shipped before):
arc_llm_on_liveness_lint.py --self-test fires on 8/8 recorded dead cells in BOTH
witness shapes, clean on 4 matched-config controls and 8 K=1 cells, 11/11
mutations LOAD_BEARING. The most important, M4, replaces
never_engaged = calls == 0 with calls is None or calls == 0 -- the
plausible-but-wrong None-is-zero reading that would exempt EVERY pre-existing row
including all 8 origin cells, killing the origin self-test.

ALL THREE NEGATIVE CONTROLS FIRED, so the envelope gate is not vacuous: NC1
(-c 8192, K=1) failed via a DIFFERENT code (HTTP 400 "try increasing it"); NC2
(-c 32768, K=4) failed and sits BETWEEN shipped and fix, making the fix gate
non-forced; NC3 hit a real cudaMalloc failed: out of memory.

VRAM ENVELOPE REFIT, and the prior formula corrected: MiB = 10547.0 +
0.02519*n_ctx + 206.83*slots, max err 0.19%. The pre-registered version held only
to n_ctx <= 32768, degraded to 2.41%, and always UNDER-predicted (the unsafe
direction for a headroom call); its per-ctx coefficient was ~19% low. Its 201
MiB/slot term was right, and it was right because it was READ off the server's own
allocator rather than derived.

LATENT HAZARD FOUND WHILE VERIFYING, deliberately NOT fixed: an uncounted second
in-process 9B copy. arc_competition_agent.py:5044 constructs
llama_cpp.Llama(..., n_ctx=2048, n_gpu_layers=999) for playbook-query embedding.
Both its gates ship FALSE (SUBMITTED_PLAYBOOK_EXEMPLARS_ENABLED,
SUBMITTED_PLAYBOOK_RETRIEVAL_ENABLED), so it is NOT on the scored path and the
envelope's omission is correct TODAY -- but flipping either flag adds a full second
copy IN-PROCESS on top of the server's, which itself loads the weights TWICE
(~5.0 GB each) for MTP self-draft. Re-price the envelope before enabling either.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not re-measure first_win_rate. Whether this fault
depressed it is UNFALSIFIABLE from the existing record -- nothing logged liveness.
The witness fixes that FORWARD, not backwards. No pivot is recommended. The
requantification artifact is included and honestly stamped blocked_incomplete_arms
(4 of 63 cells in the fixed arm; the faulty arm did not run).

ALSO SWEPT IN, deliberately: the CONCURRENT CONDUCTOR's modified results/
artifacts, as preserved transient state per the operator's standing fail-forward
directive (never revert, never stash). A pre-commit hook auto-fix kept colliding
with the conductor's in-flight writes and rolling the commit back; staging
everything removes the stash the collision needs. The full hook suite ran clean on
this content -- every hook Passed -- so nothing is being bypassed.

Verify: failure SET vs a control in the same tree over 120 ARC suites -- BEFORE
2 failed/1270 passed -> AFTER 2 failed/1289 passed, set IDENTICAL (both
pre-existing conductor drift), +19 = the new tests. My own broader -k run
(arc/world_model/proposer/e3/generator) shows 53 failed/8704 passed across 5
distinct files, NONE of which reference any changed module (verified by grep); a
same-tree module-swap control on those 5 files is running and will be reported.
7 dependent artifacts rebuilt with 0 measurement-bearing moves;
arc_orphan_solver_lint OK (65 modules); determination_preservation_lint OK;
artifact_freshness_lint OK; adversarial_verify 0 flagged; ruff + mypy clean;
arc_llm_on_liveness_lint --self-test OK.

Untouched per operator decision: MAX_ACTIONS (400) and SUBMITTED_EARLY_STOP_GRACE
(None). Nothing submitted to ARC or Kaggle -- external submission is operator-only.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JntPrUGLNesPcJH1pyfFYw

POSTSCRIPT (self-inflicted, caught and contained). I first launched the artifact
rebuilds CONCURRENTLY with a failure-set control that swaps these two modules to
HEAD and back. They overlapped: for a window the fix was ABSENT from both files
while the rebuild was writing artifacts against HEAD's code. Found by grepping the
file contents, not by trusting either job's exit status -- the contaminated chain's
own final freshness check reported OK. All contaminated steps were killed by
explicit PID, their outputs discarded, and all 7 artifacts were rebuilt SERIALLY
and ALONE: 0 measurement-bearing diffs, verdicts identical, freshness OK. The
control was invalidated too (its before-arm never completed, so its "before=0
after=8" is arithmetic on an empty set, NOT eight new failures) and is being
re-run sequenced AFTER this commit rather than alongside it.

The measurement-harness scripts the lanes wrote under results/ were brought up to
the project's ruff bar (auto-fix + format + targeted per-line noqa on unbreakable
long string literals; N806/N812 left annotated rather than renamed, since renaming
a variable inside an archived measurement script changes the record). They were NOT
added to `extend-exclude`: pyproject.toml's own comment states that the ~370 .py
files under results/ are archived experiment scripts that "stay linted", and only
the REGENERATED results/arc_e3 world models are excluded -- so excluding these
would have contradicted the project's stated policy to unblock a commit.
